1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,522
On the third day of the battle, a Roman general in a bright red cloak walks out onto a wall.

2
00:00:06,022 --> 00:00:07,297
The wall is long.

3
00:00:07,647 --> 00:00:13,936
It runs for miles in a circle around a hilltop town, and then a second wall runs around that one, facing the other way.

4
00:00:14,286 --> 00:00:17,815
Between the two walls stand tens of thousands of Roman soldiers.

5
00:00:18,315 --> 00:00:23,380
On the inside of the first wall, there is an army of Gauls, starving and trying to break out.

6
00:00:23,730 --> 00:00:28,638
On the outside of the second, there is another army, much larger, trying to break in.

7
00:00:29,138 --> 00:00:31,612
The Romans are surrounded on both sides.

8
00:00:31,962 --> 00:00:37,196
They have built a prison and a fortress at the same time, and they are standing in the gap between them.

9
00:00:37,696 --> 00:00:40,235
The man in the red cloak is Julius Caesar.

10
00:00:40,585 --> 00:00:45,507
If this wall fails, he loses an army, a war, and almost certainly his career.

11
00:00:46,007 --> 00:00:49,157
So how do you win a siege when you are the one who is besieged?

12
00:00:49,507 --> 00:00:53,531
And how does an army with a fraction of the numbers hold two enemies apart?

13
00:00:54,031 --> 00:00:57,963
The answer begins with a hill, and with a very large amount of digging.

14
00:00:58,463 --> 00:01:03,189
It is the year 52 BC, and Caesar has been fighting in Gaul for six years.

15
00:01:03,689 --> 00:01:07,934
Most of the tribes have been beaten, or bought, or frightened into obedience.

16
00:01:08,284 --> 00:01:14,963
Then, in the winter, a young nobleman of the Arverni tribe named Vercingetorix does something no Gaul had managed before.

17
00:01:15,313 --> 00:01:17,201
He gets the tribes to agree.

18
00:01:17,701 --> 00:01:19,328
Gaul is not a country.

19
00:01:19,678 --> 00:01:25,720
It is dozens of peoples with dozens of grudges, and most of them dislike each other nearly as much as they dislike Rome.

20
00:01:26,070 --> 00:01:30,848
But Vercingetorix persuades many of them to rise together, and he gives them a plan.

21
00:01:31,348 --> 00:01:33,770
Do not meet the Romans in open battle.

22
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:39,067
Burn the villages, drive off the food, cut the supply lines, and make the legions starve.

23
00:01:39,567 --> 00:01:40,908
It nearly works.

24
00:01:41,258 --> 00:01:46,414
Caesar attacks the Arvernian stronghold of Gergovia and is thrown back with heavy losses.

25
00:01:46,764 --> 00:01:52,064
It is a rare defeat for him, and it sets off a wave of rebellion among tribes that had been quiet.

26
00:01:52,414 --> 00:01:56,124
Caesar has to retreat toward the Roman territory in the south.

27
00:01:56,624 --> 00:02:00,830
On the road, Vercingetorix attacks the Roman column with his cavalry.

28
00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:03,015
This time Caesar is ready.

29
00:02:03,365 --> 00:02:07,818
His German mercenary horsemen beat the Gauls, and the Gallic army pulls back.

30
00:02:08,318 --> 00:02:12,888
Vercingetorix makes a choice here, and it is the most important choice of his life.

31
00:02:13,238 --> 00:02:15,348
He does not scatter his forces.

32
00:02:15,698 --> 00:02:25,411
He leads a large army, about eighty thousand men by Caesar's count, to the hilltop town of Alesia, the stronghold of a small tribe called the Mandubii, and he shuts himself inside.

33
00:02:25,911 --> 00:02:30,832
Caesar, who is often accused of many things but never of being slow, follows him there.

34
00:02:31,332 --> 00:02:33,689
Look at Alesia from Caesar's side.

35
00:02:34,189 --> 00:02:40,816
The town sits on top of a long hill, with steep slopes on nearly every side, and two small rivers running along the bottom.

36
00:02:41,166 --> 00:02:43,614
In front of the town there is a flat plain.

37
00:02:43,964 --> 00:02:46,646
Behind it and around it, there are more hills.

38
00:02:47,146 --> 00:02:52,458
Inside are about eighty thousand Gallic infantry, according to Caesar, plus the townspeople.

39
00:02:52,808 --> 00:02:54,891
Caesar's own army is smaller.

40
00:02:55,241 --> 00:03:04,434
We do not know exactly how much smaller, because Caesar does not tell us, but modern historians think it was perhaps fifty or sixty thousand men, including auxiliaries.

41
00:03:04,934 --> 00:03:07,486
It is a bad matchup for a direct assault.

42
00:03:07,836 --> 00:03:16,182
Storming a steep, fortified hill against an enemy who outnumbers you would cost Caesar thousands of men, and he had just seen what that looked like at Gergovia.

43
00:03:16,682 --> 00:03:21,551
So Caesar does something that looks, at first, like an admission that he is outnumbered.

44
00:03:21,901 --> 00:03:23,789
He decides not to attack.

45
00:03:24,139 --> 00:03:25,845
He decides to starve them.

46
00:03:26,345 --> 00:03:28,467
Now notice the other side of this.

47
00:03:28,817 --> 00:03:33,647
Vercingetorix has done something clever too, and it matters for the rest of the story.

48
00:03:34,147 --> 00:03:42,728
Before the Romans close the ring, he sends his cavalry out of Alesia at night, with orders to ride to every tribe in Gaul and call up every man who can carry a weapon.

49
00:03:43,228 --> 00:03:45,962
He tells them he has food for about thirty days.

50
00:03:46,462 --> 00:03:47,829
So the race is on.

51
00:03:48,179 --> 00:03:51,786
Caesar must finish a siege before a relief army can arrive.

52
00:03:52,136 --> 00:03:54,948
Vercingetorix must hold out until it does.

53
00:03:55,448 --> 00:03:59,419
And Caesar, writing about this himself, tells us what he did next.

54
00:03:59,769 --> 00:04:06,149
It is worth remembering that he wrote the account, in the third person, which is the ancient equivalent of a press release.

55
00:04:06,649 --> 00:04:09,162
The first line is the one around Alesia.

56
00:04:09,662 --> 00:04:14,284
Caesar's soldiers dig a trench twenty feet wide, with straight, vertical sides.

57
00:04:14,634 --> 00:04:18,983
Behind that, they dig two more trenches, each fifteen feet wide and fifteen deep.

58
00:04:19,333 --> 00:04:22,353
They divert a river to fill the inner one with water.

59
00:04:22,853 --> 00:04:30,770
Behind the trenches goes a rampart twelve feet high, with a wooden palisade on top, and towers at regular intervals, about every eighty feet.

60
00:04:31,270 --> 00:04:33,262
That is the part you can picture.

61
00:04:33,612 --> 00:04:36,177
The rest is what the Romans put in the ground.

62
00:04:36,677 --> 00:04:43,044
In front of the trenches, they plant rows of tree trunks with the branches sharpened into spikes, like a forest of knives.

63
00:04:43,394 --> 00:04:49,448
In front of those, they dig pits shaped like funnels, each with a sharpened stake at the bottom and covered with brushwood.

64
00:04:49,948 --> 00:04:54,779
The soldiers, with the dry humor that soldiers have in every century, called them lilies.

65
00:04:55,129 --> 00:05:00,363
And in front of those, they bury blocks of wood with iron hooks sticking up out of them, called goads.

66
00:05:00,863 --> 00:05:05,823
Any army trying to reach the wall has to cross all of this, under fire, with no cover.

67
00:05:06,323 --> 00:05:08,797
None of it stops an attack by itself.

68
00:05:09,147 --> 00:05:15,319
What it does is slow the attackers while missiles fall on them, and that buys time for reserves to reach a threatened section.

69
00:05:15,819 --> 00:05:20,884
The line around the town is about eleven Roman miles long, roughly sixteen kilometers.

70
00:05:21,234 --> 00:05:27,744
It is built in a matter of weeks, by an army that is also fighting skirmishes, foraging for food, and cutting timber.

71
00:05:28,244 --> 00:05:30,340
The scale of this is easy to miss.

72
00:05:30,690 --> 00:05:36,263
In 1861, the emperor Napoleon III ordered archaeologists to dig at the site.

73
00:05:36,613 --> 00:05:42,836
They found the ditches, and more digs in the 1990s found pits and stakes that matched Caesar's description closely.

74
00:05:43,186 --> 00:05:46,793
Whatever else he exaggerated, he did not make up the ditches.

75
00:05:47,293 --> 00:05:51,407
And then Caesar does something that no sensible commander would bother to do.

76
00:05:51,757 --> 00:05:53,463
He builds a second wall.

77
00:05:53,963 --> 00:05:58,246
The second line goes around the outside of the first, facing away from the town.

78
00:05:58,596 --> 00:06:00,549
These are not two neat circles.

79
00:06:00,899 --> 00:06:05,209
The works follow the terrain, and they link camps and strongpoints along the way.

80
00:06:05,709 --> 00:06:13,599
It is fourteen miles long, roughly twenty-one kilometers, and it has the same trenches, the same ramparts, the same towers, the same pits.

81
00:06:14,099 --> 00:06:14,815
Why?

82
00:06:15,165 --> 00:06:17,535
Because Caesar knows about the cavalry.

83
00:06:17,885 --> 00:06:20,893
He has heard what Vercingetorix told his horsemen.

84
00:06:21,243 --> 00:06:24,862
He knows a relief army is coming, and he knows it will be large.

85
00:06:25,362 --> 00:06:32,094
So he builds a wall to keep the enemy in, and then a wall to keep the other enemy out, and puts his army in the space between them.

86
00:06:32,444 --> 00:06:39,540
Caesar also orders every soldier to collect thirty days of grain and fodder, so that his own men can sit inside their own wall and wait.

87
00:06:40,040 --> 00:06:41,901
Think about what this means.

88
00:06:42,251 --> 00:06:46,704
The Romans are about to be a besieging army and a besieged army at the same time.

89
00:06:47,054 --> 00:06:49,059
They are the filling in a sandwich.

90
00:06:49,559 --> 00:06:58,166
And they have done it by digging, because Roman legionaries were trained to be engineers as much as soldiers, and the shovel was as much part of their equipment as the sword.

91
00:06:58,666 --> 00:07:06,413
Speaking of work, a like and a subscribe cost you a good deal less effort than digging a sixteen-kilometer ditch, and they keep this channel going.

92
00:07:06,913 --> 00:07:08,814
Now go back inside the town.

93
00:07:09,164 --> 00:07:11,872
Vercingetorix's thirty days are running out.

94
00:07:12,372 --> 00:07:15,731
The day the relief army was supposed to arrive comes and goes.

95
00:07:16,081 --> 00:07:18,047
Nothing appears on the horizon.

96
00:07:18,397 --> 00:07:19,959
Rations are cut again.

97
00:07:20,459 --> 00:07:25,954
At a council, one Gallic noble named Critognatus makes a speech that Caesar reports in full.

98
00:07:26,304 --> 00:07:32,788
He says that their ancestors, in a time of siege, once survived by eating the people who were too old or too young to fight.

99
00:07:33,288 --> 00:07:36,165
Nobody takes him up on it, according to Caesar.

100
00:07:36,515 --> 00:07:42,531
But the fact that anyone suggested it, if Caesar reports him accurately, tells you how bad things had become.

101
00:07:43,031 --> 00:07:45,049
The council chooses something else.

102
00:07:45,399 --> 00:07:51,128
Vercingetorix orders the Mandubii, the people who actually live in Alesia, to leave the town.

103
00:07:51,478 --> 00:07:53,574
Women, children, the old.

104
00:07:53,924 --> 00:07:56,853
There is not enough food for them and the fighting men too.

105
00:07:57,353 --> 00:08:02,939
They come out of the gates and walk toward the Roman lines, begging to be taken as slaves in exchange for food.

106
00:08:03,289 --> 00:08:05,763
Caesar says he would not let them through.

107
00:08:06,113 --> 00:08:09,251
They are left in the open ground between the two armies.

108
00:08:09,751 --> 00:08:12,407
We have only Caesar's word for any of this.

109
00:08:12,757 --> 00:08:15,452
He does not say what happened to them afterward.

110
00:08:15,952 --> 00:08:22,514
Caesar says that the Gauls who came to relieve Alesia numbered two hundred and fifty thousand infantry and eight thousand cavalry.

111
00:08:22,864 --> 00:08:26,314
Modern historians almost all think this is far too high.

112
00:08:26,664 --> 00:08:32,172
A figure of around a hundred thousand, give or take, is more commonly accepted, but nobody knows.

113
00:08:32,672 --> 00:08:37,177
Whatever the exact number, it is larger than the Roman army by a wide margin.

114
00:08:37,527 --> 00:08:39,232
And it is led by four men.

115
00:08:39,732 --> 00:08:42,141
That is the first problem for the Gauls.

116
00:08:42,491 --> 00:08:45,121
The relief force has no single commander.

117
00:08:45,471 --> 00:08:51,591
It has four leaders, chosen from different tribes, with Vercingetorix's cousin Vercassivellaunus among them.

118
00:08:51,941 --> 00:08:56,133
Each leader has his own followers, his own ideas, and his own rivals.

119
00:08:56,633 --> 00:09:00,175
The Gauls camp on a hill a mile outside the Roman outer wall.

120
00:09:00,525 --> 00:09:03,063
Vercingetorix can see them from the town.

121
00:09:03,413 --> 00:09:05,978
A cheer goes up from the starving men inside.

122
00:09:06,478 --> 00:09:08,470
Now look at the Roman position.

123
00:09:08,820 --> 00:09:12,297
The Romans are in a ring, with an army on both sides of it.

124
00:09:12,647 --> 00:09:19,391
If the two Gallic armies attack together, one from each side, the Roman defenders will be pulled in two directions at once.

125
00:09:19,891 --> 00:09:22,573
That is exactly what the Gauls intend to do.

126
00:09:22,923 --> 00:09:24,863
The problem is making it happen.

127
00:09:25,363 --> 00:09:30,076
Step back and think about the geometry, because that is where Caesar's advantage lies.

128
00:09:30,576 --> 00:09:32,881
The inner wall is eleven miles long.

129
00:09:33,231 --> 00:09:35,001
The outer wall is fourteen.

130
00:09:35,351 --> 00:09:37,396
Caesar's men are in between.

131
00:09:37,746 --> 00:09:45,623
If the Gauls attack the outer wall at one point, Caesar can send reinforcements along the inside of the ring, across the ground between the two walls.

132
00:09:45,973 --> 00:09:49,098
They only have to travel a short distance, under cover.

133
00:09:49,598 --> 00:09:54,441
The Gauls, by contrast, are split into two groups that cannot see each other's movements.

134
00:09:54,791 --> 00:09:59,101
The army outside and the army inside are separated by the whole Roman camp.

135
00:09:59,451 --> 00:10:07,432
To coordinate an attack on the same hour, they need signals, and timing, and trust between four commanders on one side and Vercingetorix on the other.

136
00:10:07,932 --> 00:10:11,487
Modern military writers call this having interior lines.

137
00:10:11,837 --> 00:10:19,011
The side in the middle can move reserves quickly from one threatened point to another, while the side on the outside has to march around the circle.

138
00:10:19,511 --> 00:10:23,235
And there is a second advantage, which is one we saw at Cannae.

139
00:10:23,585 --> 00:10:25,681
The Romans had one commander.

140
00:10:26,031 --> 00:10:30,562
At Cannae, Rome had two consuls who took turns, and one of them lost the battle.

141
00:10:31,062 --> 00:10:37,455
Here Caesar was alone, in command of everything, and he could make a decision in the time it took a rider to cross the camp.

142
00:10:37,955 --> 00:10:41,822
At Thermopylae, we saw that the number of soldiers does not count.

143
00:10:42,172 --> 00:10:44,841
What counts is how many of them can reach each other.

144
00:10:45,191 --> 00:10:48,745
At Alesia, Caesar took that idea and turned it around.

145
00:10:49,095 --> 00:10:51,140
The Gauls had enormous numbers.

146
00:10:51,490 --> 00:10:54,784
He built a shape that made it hard for them to use them together.

147
00:10:55,284 --> 00:10:59,841
The relief army arrives, and over the next three days the battle comes in three waves.

148
00:11:00,341 --> 00:11:07,176
On the first day, the Gauls fill the plain in front of the town with their cavalry and put their archers and light infantry among the horsemen.

149
00:11:07,526 --> 00:11:13,750
The Romans send out their own cavalry, and it is a long, close fight, with both sides pushing forward and back.

150
00:11:14,250 --> 00:11:18,989
In the end, Caesar's German horsemen charge together and break the Gallic cavalry.

151
00:11:19,339 --> 00:11:23,063
The relief army has lost its main strength in a single afternoon.

152
00:11:23,563 --> 00:11:29,904
On the second day, the Gauls rest, and they make ladders, hooks, and bundles of brushwood to fill the Roman ditches.

153
00:11:30,254 --> 00:11:36,178
At midnight, they attack the outer wall, shouting loudly enough that Vercingetorix can hear them from inside.

154
00:11:36,678 --> 00:11:39,634
At the same moment, his men come out of the town.

155
00:11:39,984 --> 00:11:43,968
It is the coordinated attack the Gauls have been waiting for, in the dark.

156
00:11:44,468 --> 00:11:45,509
It fails.

157
00:11:45,859 --> 00:11:51,732
Caesar's commanders, including a young officer called Mark Antony, send reserves to each threatened point.

158
00:11:52,082 --> 00:11:55,102
The Romans shower the attackers with stones and darts.

159
00:11:55,452 --> 00:12:01,220
In the darkness, many Gauls stumble into the pits and onto the goads, and are killed before they reach the wall.

160
00:12:01,720 --> 00:12:04,090
When dawn comes, they have to retreat.

161
00:12:04,440 --> 00:12:08,984
Inside the town, Vercingetorix's men have spent too long filling in the ditches.

162
00:12:09,334 --> 00:12:13,917
By the time they reach the main defenses, the relief army has already pulled back.

163
00:12:14,417 --> 00:12:16,995
Now the Gallic leaders do something clever.

164
00:12:17,345 --> 00:12:20,444
They study the Roman lines and find the one weak spot.

165
00:12:20,794 --> 00:12:27,122
To the north there is a hill, too big to be included inside the outer wall, and the Romans have built their camp on its slope.

166
00:12:27,472 --> 00:12:30,583
It is the only place the Gauls can attack from above.

167
00:12:31,083 --> 00:12:41,487
On the third day, a force of sixty thousand picked Gallic soldiers, led by Vercassivellaunus, marches out at night, hides behind the hill until noon, and comes down the slope against that camp.

168
00:12:41,837 --> 00:12:48,086
At the same time, the rest of the relief army attacks the plain, and Vercingetorix leads his men out of the town.

169
00:12:48,586 --> 00:12:50,604
This time it works, for a while.

170
00:12:50,954 --> 00:12:53,519
The Roman line on the hill begins to bend.

171
00:12:54,019 --> 00:12:57,496
Caesar is watching from a high point where he can see everything.

172
00:12:57,846 --> 00:13:02,247
He sends a general, Labienus, with six cohorts to reinforce the hill.

173
00:13:02,597 --> 00:13:10,148
When that is not enough, he goes himself, wearing the scarlet cloak that marks a Roman commander, so that every soldier on the wall can see him.

174
00:13:10,648 --> 00:13:13,929
Caesar tells us the Romans recognized him by that cloak.

175
00:13:14,279 --> 00:13:16,506
Be a little careful with that detail.

176
00:13:16,856 --> 00:13:20,202
It makes a good picture, and he was the one writing the book.

177
00:13:20,552 --> 00:13:26,203
What mattered more was the reserves he moved to the threatened section, and the cavalry he sent around the back.

178
00:13:26,703 --> 00:13:28,760
And Caesar has one more move.

179
00:13:29,110 --> 00:13:34,813
He sends his cavalry out of a gate, around the outside of the outer wall, to hit the Gauls from behind.

180
00:13:35,163 --> 00:13:38,652
The Gauls on the hill see horsemen behind them, and they break.

181
00:13:39,002 --> 00:13:40,968
Vercassivellaunus is captured.

182
00:13:41,468 --> 00:13:44,879
When the army outside breaks, the army inside gives up.

183
00:13:45,229 --> 00:13:48,198
Vercingetorix leads his men back into the town.

184
00:13:48,548 --> 00:13:49,667
It is over.

185
00:13:50,167 --> 00:13:53,227
The next morning, Vercingetorix calls a council.

186
00:13:53,577 --> 00:13:58,590
He tells the Gallic chiefs that he did not fight for his own sake, but for the freedom of Gaul.

187
00:13:58,940 --> 00:14:03,119
He says they can kill him or hand him over to Caesar, and let Caesar decide.

188
00:14:03,619 --> 00:14:06,041
They send messengers to the Roman camp.

189
00:14:06,391 --> 00:14:12,029
Caesar demands that the leaders and weapons be given up, and he sits in front of his camp to receive them.

190
00:14:12,529 --> 00:14:18,153
Plutarch, writing about a hundred and fifty years later, gives us a famous picture of what happened next.

191
00:14:18,503 --> 00:14:25,079
He says Vercingetorix put on his best armor, decorated his horse, rode out of the gate, and circled around Caesar.

192
00:14:25,429 --> 00:14:29,842
Then he dismounted, threw down his weapons, and sat silently at Caesar's feet.

193
00:14:30,342 --> 00:14:32,308
We do not know if that is true.

194
00:14:32,658 --> 00:14:34,299
But it is a good story.

195
00:14:34,799 --> 00:14:38,106
Caesar gave each of his soldiers one prisoner as booty.

196
00:14:38,456 --> 00:14:41,841
He kept the prisoners of two tribes to win those tribes back.

197
00:14:42,341 --> 00:14:45,791
Vercingetorix was imprisoned in Rome for about six years.

198
00:14:46,141 --> 00:14:51,467
In 46 BC, he was paraded in Caesar's triumph through the city, and then executed.

199
00:14:51,967 --> 00:14:54,037
Alesia broke the great revolt.

200
00:14:54,387 --> 00:14:58,410
Fighting went on in Gaul afterward, but nothing came close to this again.

201
00:14:58,760 --> 00:15:04,307
Gaul was Roman for the next five hundred years, and the language that grew out of it is still spoken today.

202
00:15:04,807 --> 00:15:08,413
For Caesar, it was the foundation of everything that came next.

203
00:15:08,763 --> 00:15:14,258
The Gallic conquest made him rich, famous, and the commander of a veteran army that was loyal to him.

204
00:15:14,608 --> 00:15:19,998
Three years later, he would lead that army across a small river in northern Italy into a civil war.

205
00:15:20,498 --> 00:15:22,568
For the Gauls, it was the end.

206
00:15:22,918 --> 00:15:27,163
They had risen together, but the relief army never had a single command.

207
00:15:27,663 --> 00:15:30,722
And remember the thing that Vercingetorix did right.

208
00:15:31,072 --> 00:15:34,106
He found a strong position and drew the enemy to it.

209
00:15:34,456 --> 00:15:37,464
What went wrong for the Gauls was not Vercingetorix.

210
00:15:37,814 --> 00:15:40,925
It was the shape of the battle that Caesar built around him.

211
00:15:41,425 --> 00:15:44,277
It also shows what the wall could and could not do.

212
00:15:44,627 --> 00:15:49,392
The ditches and ramparts delayed the attacks and let smaller groups hold sections of the line.

213
00:15:49,742 --> 00:15:54,586
But Caesar still needed reserves, local commanders and cavalry to stop a breakthrough.

214
00:15:54,936 --> 00:15:57,058
The fortifications gave him time.

215
00:15:57,408 --> 00:15:59,699
His army still had to fight to use it.

216
00:16:00,199 --> 00:16:02,543
Alesia is a story about a wall.

217
00:16:02,893 --> 00:16:05,523
But it is not the usual kind of wall story.

218
00:16:06,023 --> 00:16:10,280
A wall, usually, protects the people behind it from the people in front.

219
00:16:10,630 --> 00:16:14,146
At Alesia, the same earth and wood did two things at once.

220
00:16:14,496 --> 00:16:16,917
For the Gauls inside, it was a prison.

221
00:16:17,267 --> 00:16:20,340
For the Romans between the walls, it was a fortress.

222
00:16:20,690 --> 00:16:23,685
The difference was which side of the ditch you were standing on.

223
00:16:24,185 --> 00:16:26,255
Look at the three battles together.

224
00:16:26,605 --> 00:16:30,185
At Thermopylae, a narrow place made a small army strong.

225
00:16:30,535 --> 00:16:33,907
At Cannae, a crowded place made a large army helpless.

226
00:16:34,257 --> 00:16:39,973
At Alesia, a built place made a small army safe from an army that outnumbered it several times over.

227
00:16:40,473 --> 00:16:46,619
Each time, the winner was the one who understood the space, and made the other side fight in a shape they could not use.

228
00:16:47,119 --> 00:16:49,970
If you take one thing from Alesia, take this.

229
00:16:50,320 --> 00:16:54,122
Numbers decide nothing until they are in the same place at the same time.

230
00:16:54,472 --> 00:16:56,829
Caesar made sure the Gauls never were.
